{:task "seon.ctx/a-turns-diff-tells-an-eval-by-its-handle" :merge "852239c44" :plan-at "parent" :plan "With told? deleted (seon.ctx/the-agent-changes-its-task-by-swap), each turn's :ctx diff (seon.context/world) tells every path that changed, an eval the agent made among them, whole: its :seon.eval/record (code, response, :fns) is told again though the transcript already holds it. Measured, an outside agent's one swap! between two commits: the turn message 467 chars with told? (the page part alone, the plan's path not told as data) and 2,504 without it, of which about 2,000 the eval's record; [:seon.agent/id] is told too. The ruling \"The render of ctx never repeats what an eval shows\" carries the eval's handle where the render touches an eval; the diff should do the same: a member of :evals told as its handle (result/eN) and the paths under it that changed after it was made (an outside fact such as :seon.eval/opened), at the producer (seon.ctx/diff's caller, world), never by a filter of paths. Why :seon.agent/id moves between two of the agent's commits is a defect in what ctx holds, to find first." :fns ["seon.agent/begin-run!" "seon.context/request" "seon.context/turn-pages" "seon.context/world"] :sections ["docs/agent.md#ctx" "docs/prompt.md#you-are-a-seon-agent"] :schemas [] :tasks []}
{:task "seon.proofs/prove-is-the-one-runner" :merge "c72bed796" :plan-at "parent" :plan "Root's audit (audit-dup-system, item 1, with the cold review's cuts), 2026-10-27: seon.prove admits and records every test run, but two callers bypassed it and the process tests had two filters and a second reading of results. Done: my.agent/prove asks the service (seon.agent/prove! :judge; given proofs, a run of their namespaces, :names now forwarded by seon.agent-proc), so an agent's REPL run is recorded on main and is one run with a turn's of the same head; seon.root/run-tests! asks it too (seon.root/prove! in mode :judge; its ticks arity went, the ROBUST check's tick-bound case evaluates seon.agent/run-tests-forked in the agent); one process filter, seon.proofs/left-out, for bin/test's plan and seon.agent/run-selection (test-runner/automatic deleted); the merge gate's :tests effect reads each test's verdict at the digest prove's plan wrote (out/tests.edn) in the results directory, not prove's printed RED/SHARD lines (fx/test-results' regex deleted); and the process tests a change reaches gate its merge with nobody asking (owner's ruling 2026-10-27; seon.reconcile no longer reads :seon.task/gate?, whose code stays until THE PLAN step 0.7 exercises the gate). The gate's checkout, undeclared, is the root's own source root (seon.root/start!), and a root with neither holds such a merge with its reason. Not done: the dashboard's run-tests! (task seon.proofs/the-dashboards-run-tests-asks-the-service)." :fns ["my.agent/deferred" "my.agent/prove" "my.root/gate!" "seon.agent-proc/agent-frame" "seon.agent/prove!" "seon.agent/run-selection" "seon.agent/run-tests-forked" "seon.context/turn-state" "seon.fork-server/graph-at-fork" "seon.fork-server/server-step" "seon.fx/run-tests" "seon.fx/test-results" "seon.program/moves-program?" "seon.proofs/left-out" "seon.proofs/status-at" "seon.prove/answered" "seon.prove/prove-proc" "seon.prove/request" "seon.prove/run-key" "seon.prove/slot" "seon.reconcile/actions" "seon.reconcile/done-explain" "seon.reconcile/start" "seon.root/prove!" "seon.root/run-tests!" "seon.root/start!"] :sections ["docs/failure-points.md#edges-that-catch-each-names-its-one-variant" "docs/merging.md#a-merge-runs-what-the-change-reaches" "docs/merging.md#the-test-service" "docs/tasks.md#a-request-is-one-transaction" "docs/turn.md#who-runs-which-tests" "docs/vocabulary.md#data-datahikes-and-gits-words"] :schemas [":seon.repl/ticks-spent" ":seon.task/evidence"] :tasks ["seon.proofs/a-prove-runs-key-does-not-move-between-two-asks" "seon.proofs/an-agent-reads-its-own-recorded-results" "seon.proofs/main-is-proved-in-a-fork" "seon.proofs/the-dashboards-run-tests-asks-the-service"]}
{:task "seon.cost/a-call-allocates-the-same-bytes-each-time" :merge "79bcdf069" :plan-at "parent" :plan "Bytes are exact (AGENTS 5), yet seon.merge-cost-test/a-three-way-costs-the-changes-not-the-program went red once (n^0.92 against 0.91) and green on an equal tree: the same call on the same input allocated a few hundred bytes more or less (seon.merge/delta-three-way 3,709,584-3,713,248 B at n=300). Found so far, measured: a pure function is exact; each Datahike read alone is near exact; in bare Jolt with this thread's count (Chez thread_bytes_allocated, which our fork already uncounts for a collection and its handler, s/7.ss $call-uncounted) one thread's work is exact through collections (one +128 B in 40), but with a second thread allocating, a stack-deep lazy read (subseq x100 on a sorted set) gains +96 or +192 B on runs a collection lands in, while a flat one (vector build) stays exact; uncounting Chez's collect rendezvous changes nothing. Not a stack split: uncounting all of c/schsig.c's S_split_and_resize and S_overflow's split continuation (S_thread_bytes_uncount around them, as alloc.c uncounts a new stack) left it as it was (subseq x100: +96 B on 7-8 of 40 runs, measured twice on a Chez built with it); reverted. What holds: always +96 B (+192 rarely), only on runs a collection lands in, only with another thread allocating, only in the stack-deep lazy read, never single-threaded. Candidates, untested: (a) THE NEXT STEP: the event path, a thread brought to another thread's collection by its event check mid-call going through $event and the collect request's dispatch outside docollect's uncount (a lone thread never waits: it collects itself); read per run the fork's event and collect-request counters and correlate with the +96; (b) a one-shot continuation (a lazy seq's, a try's) promoted to multi-shot by another thread's collection and copied on reinstatement (S_promote_to_multishot, the underflow copy); (c) the allocation area's refill after another thread's collection (gc.c:939 drops each thread's tail) charging a header. Not a try: one try/catch entry costs 176 B at stack depths 0, 100, 200 and 400, in bare Jolt and in a Seon test process alike. Ruled out also: contracts, the tick timer, Jolt's runtime hashtables and string-hash cache, Datahike's search and node caches. Correct: a call's bytes are what it allocates, whatever collections land in it and on whatever thread. Proof: one measured call repeated 40 times with another thread allocating gives one byte count." :fns ["seon.cost/measure"] :sections ["docs/config.md#constants" "docs/cost.md#what-is-measured"] :schemas [":seon.config.proofs/cost-max-samples" ":seon.config.proofs/cost-settled" ":seon.cost/unsettled" ":seon.profile/fn-cost"] :tasks []}
{:task "seon.fx/the-source-tree-follows-main" :merge "8483f68b9" :plan-at "merge" :plan "Found 2026-10-27 by root (approved the same day; branch jolt/the-source-tree-follows-main, nothing built yet, only this entry). Symptom: the standing root runs main's new binary after every merge, but main's program in runs/store held seon.repl/draw-part (build 3 deleted it) and no vars of seon.ratom, so agents forking from root's main get a stale program, and the page shows problems in deleted code (an unnamed catch site in draw-part, 52 dead citations). Cause: main's program is imported from the write-back tree, not the checkout: config/local.edn sets :seon.config.source/root to .worktrees/merges (branch jolt/merges), and only bin/update's step 2 (git -C .worktrees/merges merge --ff-only main) moves it. The jolt/merges reflog shows that fast-forward on every merge until 2026-10-26 20:04 (4436167591, 'merge-log: a fresh root loads its task files') and none after: that night's merges restarted root with bin/root and never ran bin/update, so jolt/merges stood 262 commits behind main (0 commits of its own). Boot's catch-up (seon.root/import-source!) runs every restart and reads (:root source), the frozen tree, faithfully; the source fact even says so (:seon.root/source :unshared 'the binary was built from tree c4aa40e6..., the source is tree 4bc70d16...'), which nothing reads. The narrowed watch (2e4ae8d360, 20:49) came after the freeze and is not the cause, but there is a second defect beside it: my.root/loader-dirs and the own ref use (config/checkout), the main tree, while the importer reads the source root. So a merge into main moves refs/heads/main and fires a :source load that re-imports the unchanged write-back tree, and a commit in the write-back tree (Seon's own write-back) is not the checkout's ref and fires nothing. Unblocked by hand: root ran bin/update (0 jolt/merges commits to merge, jolt/merges fast-forwarded to main, rebuilt, restarted). Correct, at the cause (root's go, 2026-10-27): the source tree follows main inside the :source effect (seon.fx/read-source, called by seon.root/import-source! at boot and on each :source load). When the write-back branch is an ancestor of the checkout's main, it is fast-forwarded first (through the :git owner, an effect, in the write-back tree). When it holds commits main lacks (Seon's merges not yet in main), it is imported as now and the gap is a recorded problem naming both shas and both counts, never silent. The watch watches the source root's dirs and fires on main's ref and on the write-back branch's ref, so the watch and the import name one tree: loader-dirs' :source from the source root; :docs and :tasks stay the checkout's. Deletes bin/update's step 2 (steps 1 and 3-5 stay). Estimate src +40/-10 (seon.fx, seon.root, my.root, bin/update). Proofs, red first: an e2e on its own clone (as seon.source-test/a-restart-catches-up-and-a-new-generation-resumes builds one, with its own results dir) where a commit to the clone's main that defines a var reaches main's program while root runs (the var's entity is there after the ref moves) and across a restart; a write-back branch holding a commit main lacks is a recorded problem naming both shas; and my.root/watch-loads fires :source for a path under the source root and for the write-back branch's ref." :fns ["my.root/loader-dirs" "my.root/source-root" "my.root/watch!" "my.root/watch-loads" "seon.fx/follow-main" "seon.fx/read-source" "seon.root/import-source!"] :sections ["docs/failure-points.md#edges-that-catch-each-names-its-one-variant" "docs/source.md#import-the-running-generations-source-as-mains-program"] :schemas [] :tasks ["seon.fx/a-tree-run-is-one-function"]}
