{:task "seon.reconcile/an-index-is-observed-at-the-cost-of-what-changed" :merge "ac930a4b6" :plan-at "parent" :plan "Observing main's index (```seon.reconcile/index``` over a commit, the reconciler's :main step) costs about 8.3M ticks and 65 MB whatever the commit changed: it takes a datahike.api/datoms-diff of each of the 74 watched attributes' ranges, and on the test store (no node kept at an address) each diff walks the attribute's datoms as the index lays them out. So its cost is the store's layout, not the change, and its proof (seon.reconcile-test/an-index-is-observed-at-the-cost-of-what-changed) cannot see growth below n=1000. Measured by seon.cost/measure of r/index on seon.reconcile-test/observed-input, in one process (n=10 repeated three times, identical to a few hundred ticks; not warm-up), on main's tree: n=10 8.18M ticks 64.2 MB, n=100 8.44M 63.2 MB, n=1000 10.92M 65.3 MB, 17 calls each; with one more schema attribute (:seon.task/sections): n=10 8.33M 65.5 MB, n=100 8.43M 63.2 MB, n=1000 10.86M 64.9 MB. n=10 allocates more than n=100 on both, and n=500 costs less than n=10 on both (7.96M and 7.90M ticks, 52.2 MB and 51.8 MB). Correct: a commit's datoms decide which attributes' ranges are read (the attributes its datoms name, of the watched ones), so an unrelated commit costs what it changed and nothing else; then the proof's sizes go back to [10 100 1000] (they are [10 500 1000] meanwhile: its contract asks three) and its fixed part falls to what one changed attribute costs." :fns [] :sections ["docs/cost.md#how-we-measure"] :schemas [] :tasks ["seon.reconcile/one-observe-descends-once"]}
