{:task "seon.watch/root-watches-what-its-loaders-read" :merge "71c30bd41" :plan-at "merge" :plan "Found 2026-10-26 (root's question: fseventsd, pid 81742, has held a core since about 10-23). Measured, read-only: fseventsd uses ~62 CPU-seconds a minute at ~127 file events a second system-wide (a 20 s FSEvents stream on /: 2,540 events), about 8 ms of its CPU an event, so it is saturated: today's watcher runs measured deliveries 5 to 18 s late under load. Its cumulative CPU (6,566 min in 12.4 days since its start on 10-14, 37% on average) means the pinned stretch began around 10-22/23, which is when seon.watch arrived (3565b72e45, 10-23), not when fseventsd started. What Seon asks of it: my.root/watch! watches the whole checkout and the repository's refs with per-file events (native/watch.c: FileEvents, NoDefer, WatchRoot, latency 0). For the main tree that checkout holds every worktree (.worktrees/*), each with target/ (builds, shards, check runs, lmdb-sem's 65,536-file search), runs/ (LMDB stores) and .git/modules: of the 20 s sample, ~85% of the volume's events were under /Users/sean/src/seon-next, and every one is delivered to the main root's stream, though watch-loads reads none of them (a batch no loader reads writes nothing). Every test root and watch test starts a stream of its own over its checkout too: 9,673 new fseventsd clients in the 6 hours to 20:30 (3,454 and 4,308 in the two busiest hours, much of it this session's watcher loops), each, since d28e10a585, starting from an event id, so fseventsd runs a history scan (scan_old) for each. Not proven: how fseventsd's per-event cost depends on its clients (removed clients are not logged, and its client list needs root), so whether stale clients add to it is open. Correct, at the cause: root watches what its loaders read, my.root/loader-dirs (docs, the task dir, the source dirs) and the refs, never the checkout, so the churn of builds, stores and other worktrees never reaches a stream; watch-loads' :rescan and :root handling is unchanged (a rescan above a loader dir still reads it). Measured before and after, on the same machine: fseventsd's CPU a minute and the events delivered to the main root's stream a minute (its :seon.root/watch fact's :paths), and a watcher's ready-to-delivery lag. The owner decides whether fseventsd is restarted for a clean baseline. Replaces nothing; deletes the checkout root from watch!." :fns ["my.root/loader" "my.root/watch!" "my.root/watch-loads" "seon.main/root-serve" "seon.watch/start!"] :sections ["docs/health.md#every-writer-root-loads-what-changed"] :schemas [] :tasks ["seon.watch/a-watchers-end-waits-on-its-own-exit"]}
