{:task "seon.cost/a-contract-carries-its-cost-bound" :merge "eea2b740b" :plan-at "merge" :plan "The owner's direction (2026-10-26, after notes/cost-bounds-2026-10-26.md): a design writes data, the backbone functions declared with their contracts and bounds before their bodies exist, and an implement task is done when every declaration holds. Step 1: `:seon.cost/bound` is a contract key beside `:malli/schema`, (fn [& args] {:ticks :bytes :calls :out}), read by ```seon.cost/bound```. A cost proof gains a generated form (`:seon.cost/generated`): ```seon.cost/cases``` draws argument vectors from a test.check generator or the contract's input (malli), at the test's seed, and ```seon.cost/run``` measures each against the bound on the same arguments, judged by ```seon.cost/judge``` at the same slack. Growth is not fitted, since the bound states it. This is one mechanism: the same run, measure and judge, with no second runner. Proved on ```seon.ctx/changes```, whose bound sketches its walk (```seon.ctx/walk-of```) priced at per-step costs measured once (docs/cost.md#a-bound-in-the-contract), and a copy without its identical? skip is red under the same bound. Step 2 follows on its own branch: declaring a var with its contract and bound and no body from the REPL, visible in ctx, and the bound-graph render." :fns ["seon.cost/bound" "seon.cost/cases" "seon.cost/expected" "seon.cost/explain" "seon.cost/growth" "seon.cost/judge" "seon.cost/per-unit" "seon.cost/proof" "seon.cost/run" "seon.ctx/change-steps" "seon.ctx/changes" "seon.ctx/changes-bound" "seon.ctx/walk-of"] :sections ["docs/cost.md#a-bound-in-the-contract" "docs/resolution.md#sanctioned"] :schemas [":seon.config.proofs/case-sizes" ":seon.cost/estimate" ":seon.cost/generated" ":seon.cost/no-bound" ":seon.cost/row" ":seon.cost/sized" ":seon.cost/unit"] :tasks ["seon.cost/a-cost-proof-is-the-same-under-load" "seon.cost/a-db-function-draws-its-cases-from-a-domain-generator"]}
{:task "seon.fork-server/every-fork-server-wait-ends-with-the-server" :merge "50be95c4d" :plan-at "parent" :plan "Found 2026-10-25 by the stability sweep (notes/stability-waits-2026-10-25.md), checked against the code. Three waits on the fork server end only on their answer. (1) `seon.fork-server/promote` with no live standby answers :fork-server-lost and keeps :waiters: each queued :ext request (:stats, :static) is never answered, and a request arriving after the loss is queued with nothing to send it to (:ext sends only when :pid). The loss must answer every waiter, and every later request, with the declared error (docs/failure-points.md, the fork server). (2) :stats and :static answers pair with :waiters by FIFO (`peek`). After a promote the standby is sent every unanswered request again, so FIFO holds only while every answer the dead server wrote arrived whole before its EOF; an answer cut by the death pairs the next waiter with the wrong request. The request carries its :rid to the server and the answer echoes it, and the proc answers the waiter by :rid. (3) `seon.fork-server/release!` waits for the boot server's exit with a blocking `c-waitpid` (no WNOHANG), so a wedged server (a deadlocked child, docs/failure-points.md#physics-who-may-run-threads) hangs root's stop; then `.join 5000` on the event reader is a clock deciding (\u00a74). Stop is a kill of the server's process group (as a root's processes end with it, AGENTS.md \u00a78) followed by the reap and the reader's EOF, and no timeout. Deletes the join's timeout; replaces FIFO pairing with :rid pairing." :fns ["seon.fork-server/fork-server-proc" "seon.fork-server/lost" "seon.fork-server/promote" "seon.fork-server/release!" "seon.root/back-op"] :sections ["docs/failure-points.md#failure-points-met-without-a-catch-the-edge-answers-data"] :schemas [] :tasks ["seon.fork-server/an-answer-pairs-by-request-and-stop-ends-a-wedged-server"]}
