{:task "datahike.db/a-time-predicate-read-keeps-index-order" :merge "60868f518" :plan-at "parent" :plan "Found by worktree session ctx-atom (2026-10-26) while building pull's :reverse option (Datahike 872bfa03, docs/forks.md#datahike). A db read through a time predicate (as-of, since) answers its datoms out of index order once a read spans more than eight [e a] groups, and its rseek-datoms realizes the index from the seek point down to the index's start. Reproduction, on a :keep-history? store: an entity root and ten children, each written in its own transaction with [:parent root]; as of the sixth child's transaction, (d/datoms asof :avet :parent root) answers six datoms, and (take 6 (d/rseek-datoms asof :avet :parent root)) answers [7 9 9 \"c5\" 6 \u2026], out of order and leaving the [:parent root] prefix (with four children both answer in order). Cause: datahike.db/post-process-datoms, for a context with a time predicate and not historical, transduces the datoms into a map grouped by [e a] (group-by-step) and assembles each group's current datoms (assemble-datoms-xform): Clojure's array map keeps insertion order up to eight entries and a hash map after, so the answer's order is the hash's; the transduce is eager, so over rseek-datoms' open range (down to the index's start) it reads everything below the seek point. Anything reading an as-of or since db in index order (a pull's collection, a seek, a newest-n window, a merge of two sorted reads) can be wrong. Correct, in Datahike on a jolt/<topic> branch: a time-predicate read keeps index order (each [e a] group's current datoms emitted where the group starts, in the order the groups were read) and stays lazy, so a seek or rseek reads what it is taken for. Pull's :reverse sorts the bounded prefix on such a db (datahike.pull-api/reverse-datoms), so its :limit n there reads the whole collection; this task deletes that sort branch and its datahike.datom require, leaving rseek-datoms the one read. Proof: datahike.test.time-predicate-order-test/a-time-predicate-read-keeps-index-order (ten children as of the sixth: datoms and rseek-datoms in index order, rseek ends at the prefix), and Datahike's own suite answering the same before and after." :fns [] :sections ["docs/forks.md#datahike"] :schemas [] :tasks []}
