{:task "seon.db/a-store-is-made-whole-or-not-at-all" :merge "d4ecad47f" :plan-at "merge" :plan "Found by the full run of 2026-10-26 (main 6929ca4e9, shard-0): a store's create that failed after its LMDB env had made data.mdb (MDB_SEM_UNDO_FULL, the env's first write) left a file holding no database at the path. `seon.db/open!` takes the data file for the store (exists?), so it never creates it again, and every later open connects and reads \"Database does not exist\"; konserve refuses to create over the file (\"LMDB store already exists\"), so no open recovers it. Correct (root's ruling, 2026-10-26): the create is atomic. `seon.db/open!` builds a new store in a sibling temporary directory (the database created, seeded and synced there, its connection released so its env closes) and renames it to the path: a rename is atomic on one filesystem, so the path holds a whole store or none. A failed create leaves only its temporary directory; two creators race only on the rename, and the loser removes its own and opens the winner's. A store is named by the id it records, never its path (`seon.db/store-cfg`, `seon.db-test/a-moved-store-opens`), so the renamed store opens at the path. This removes the half-made state rather than recovering from it; docs/failure-points.md#lmdb-robust-locks's manual removal goes. Proof: seon.db-test/a-create-that-fails-leaves-no-store, a create made to fail after its env file exists (the process's undo entries exhausted, so the env opens and its first write is refused): red, the path holds a data file and the next open reads \"Database does not exist\"; green, the path is missing and the next open creates a whole store." :fns ["seon.db/make!" "seon.db/open!"] :sections ["docs/failure-points.md#edges-that-catch-each-names-its-one-variant" "docs/failure-points.md#lmdb-robust-locks"] :schemas [":seon.db/store-unplaced"] :tasks ["seon.db/a-one-eval-commit-costs-what-it-writes"]}
