{:task "seon.vector/a-text-written-on-a-branch-is-embedded-there" :merge "ae983ec64" :plan-at "parent" :plan "What exists (docs/vector.md#what-is-embedded): forms, sections and task plans are embedded on main by `seon.vector/vectors-proc` at the writer's commit notice; an agent's branch reads main's vectors as of its fork, so a plan the agent rewrites with `my.task/update!`, a function it defines, or a section text it changes has no vector until it merges. So `my.agent/similar` on its own new function answers [], and anything ranked by the plan's vector (the story's :similar, the related sections of `seon.context/the-screen-shows-the-sections-nearest-the-plan`) cannot follow the plan as the agent refines it over its run. An agent already reaches the model: `my.agent/similar` asks root's :embed effect over its socket (`agent/fx! :embed`), and the answer is plain data. The change: at the end of each turn's evals, the agent computes ```seon.vector/due``` on its branch (texts whose vector is missing or made from an earlier text: the plan changed, a new form) restricted to what the branch wrote since its fork, asks :embed once for all of them (batched as the effect already batches), and transacts ```seon.vector/tx-data``` on its branch; a failed embedding is the effect's recorded error and the texts stay due. Main is unchanged: a branch's vectors merge with its entities (check that the merge carries `:seon.vector/*` facts, or that main re-embeds the merged texts, whichever the merge already does; say which). No new attribute, no thread in the agent (the model stays in root). Cost proof: a turn that writes nothing due asks nothing." :fns ["my.agent/query-of" "my.agent/related" "my.agent/similar" "my.task/related" "seon.agent/embed-written!" "seon.agent/fx!" "seon.context/by-frequency" "seon.context/related" "seon.context/related-at" "seon.context/related-count" "seon.context/related-view" "seon.context/turn-state" "seon.program/meaning" "seon.program/quoted-var?" "seon.pr