{:task "seon.cost/ticks-are-exact" :merge "75fa45bf2" :plan-at "merge" :plan "Found 2026-10-23 (worktree session store) under seon.source-test/a-fixed-change-costs-the-same-at-any-program-size: the transact row's ticks differed between runs, 406.8k / 425k / 479.6k at 10 / 100 / 1,000 in one bin/prove shard against 406.3k / 424.8k / 477.1k in another process, 0.5% at 1,000, enough to move a growth fit by 0.02. docs/cost.md calls ticks exact. Reproduction (scratch script, seon.profile/measure! of d/db-with on seon.source-test/transact-of 10, 40 samples per process): within a process the count is exact, 406,355-406,362 per sample. The first call reads 409,285 (warming, which measure's warm call takes). About one sample in 40 reads 1,000 more (Chez's timer quantum, docs/cost.md). Holding 300 MB of live data first changes nothing (no collection ran during any sample). Two causes. (1) Fixed: seon.cost/measure repeated a call until 20 ms of CPU time or 25 calls, a clock deciding how many samples a counted dimension's minimum is taken over: a fast call took 25 and a slow or loaded one 3. It now takes seon.cost/repeats (3) after its warm call, whatever a call costs (seon.cost-test/a-measure-takes-a-count-of-samples-never-a-time: 26 calls before, 4 after). (2) Not yet found: the shard's process counts 0.1-0.5% more ticks for the same call than a fresh one. It is not the heap, and not a collection. It is what ran earlier in that process, e.g. protocol-dispatch caches (Jolt's PIC and resolve-memo, invalidated by every registration), Datahike's global caches, or the store's id counters giving other entity ids and so other index paths. Next: measure the row in one fresh process after requiring and running each other test namespace of its shard, bisect to the one that moves the count, and fix at that state's owner, so the same call counts the same in any process." :fns ["seon.cost/measure" "seon.cost/repeats"] :sections ["docs/cost.md#what-is-measured"] :schemas [] :tasks ["seon.cost/cost-tolerances-are-derived"]}
