{:task "seon.task/a-runs-lifecycle-is-read-once" :merge "2e21e5e43" :plan-at "merge" :plan "Owner, 2026-10-23: src resists growth so agents reuse and improve the core parts rather than making one-offs (DECISIONS). Found by the one-offs survey (notes/one-offs-2026-10-23.md, item 1). What existed: a run's lifecycle sets (:seon.run/status's finished, judged and failed, declared on its schema) read by seon.reconcile, seon.problems and seon.render.ui each; whether a task is done computed by the reconciler from its index and again by seon.problems/task-done? on the db; and seon.server's run-of and :context op reading every run on main (task/runs) for the one running. Correct: seon.task, below them all, reads the sets once (finished, judged, failed) and owns done? (one AVET seek of a task's runs) and running (one seek of the :running status); the others require them. A law keeps the sets read once: no src form outside seon.task calls a schema reader (properties, enum-values) on :seon.run/status. Deletes the three readers, problems' task-done? and the server's whole-main scans." :fns ["seon.problems/death-statuses" "seon.problems/run-pull" "seon.problems/run-rows" "seon.reconcile/finished" "seon.reconcile/judged" "seon.render.ui/finished" "seon.server/ops" "seon.server/run-of" "seon.task/done?" "seon.task/failed" "seon.task/finished" "seon.task/judged" "seon.task/running"] :sections ["docs/render.md#tiles-are-data-the-context-reads-the-pages-derivations"] :schemas [] :tasks ["seon.task/design-writes-the-implement-steps-proofs"]}
{:task "seon.lib/real-mb-reads-what-macos-charges" :merge "77679b969" :plan-at "parent" :plan "What exists: `bin/check scale`'s root gauge, `seon.lib/real-mb`, read 398-404 MB for a root whose macOS phys_footprint (`footprint PID`) was 1,460 MB with the embedding model open, and 856 MB on Seon's own program without it (measured 2026-10-23 on jolt/similar-finds-helpers-by-embedding). A memory bound that reads low cannot hold a 16 GB machine: `:seon.config.machine/memory-mb` is judged on a quarter of the truth. Correct: the gauge reads the task's phys_footprint (task_info TASK_VM_INFO, what Activity Monitor and jetsam charge), and scale's need uses it for root, the fork server and each agent. Proof: on a root holding a 300 MB malloc'd block, the gauge grows by about 300 MB and agrees with footprint within 5%." :fns ["seon.agent/mem-mb" "seon.lib/mp-probe" "seon.lib/real-mb"] :sections ["docs/cost.md#how-we-measure" "docs/failure-points.md#failure-points-met-without-a-catch-the-edge-answers-data" "docs/machine.md#the-target-a-16-gb-macbook-m1"] :schemas [":seon.lib/probe"] :tasks ["seon.agent/memory-is-read-when-asked"]}
