{:task "seon.check/health-is-what-a-branch-caused" :merge "95eac515f" :plan-at "merge" :plan "Root's ruling, 2026-10-23, on the walk session's finding (the owner asked: \"why is health not useful? that's a problem\"). What happens: in a worktree whose config keys differ from main's, `bin/check health` exits 1 with no output and no verdict line. Two causes stack. bin/worktree copies main's target/seon, compiled with main's config schema, so it refuses the tree's resources/seon/config/default.edn (\"wiki-baseline missing-key, turn-text-budget unknown-key\"), and nothing compares target/seon.tree with the tree it runs on. Then bin/check's single mode runs `run seed 2000 > /dev/null || exit 1`, so its error is thrown away (AGENTS section 9: every check ends on X OK / X FAILED). And even when it runs, the step prints the tree's absolute open rows (seon.problems/health), which were red on an unchanged main when it was added (10 dead citations, 01fd0496), while a Seon agent is held only to what its branch caused since its merge base (seon.problems/caused, the done rule). Build: (1) bin/check never hides a step: a failed seed or drop prints its output and MODE FAILED. A driver mode of the binary (seon.main) refuses, saying which tree it was built from and which it runs on, when target/seon.tree is not the checkout's tree (seon.fx/tree-id), rather than checking with a stale binary. (2) The health step is what a branch caused: seon.problems/caused-split of the base against the checkout. The base is the branch's merge base with git's main; with main checked out and a clean tree, its first parent (the previous main, so a merge that adds problems is red); with main checked out and uncommitted changes, HEAD (seon.fx/base-commit). The base db is the head's with the base tree's program, wiki and tasks loaded by root's own loaders (seon.source/replace-tx, seon.docs/load-dir-tx, seon.task/load-tx), from the base tree extracted by git (seon.fx/checkout-at). Its result is keyed by the tree and the base's tree (seon.proofs/step-digests), so an unchanged pair reuses it. bin/laws catch reads the same step for :unnamed-site. Absolute health stays root's to show live (root's context and page lead with it); the merge session reads it through the same step with the previous main as base, so there is no second path. AGENTS section 3's \"run bin/check health\" lines say so. Deletes: the absolute verdict of bin/check health." :fns ["seon.fx/base-commit" "seon.fx/checkout-at" "seon.fx/tree-id" "seon.fx/tree-of" "seon.problems/caused-split" "seon.proofs/check-steps" "seon.proofs/step-digests"] :sections ["docs/checks.md#modes" "docs/failure-points.md#edges-that-catch-each-names-its-one-variant" "docs/health.md#checks-are-callers-of-seon"] :schemas [":seon.config.source/main"] :tasks []}
