{:task "seon.render/walk" :merge "49c4aec75" :plan-at "parent" :plan "The one mechanism every view of Seon's data goes through: the owner's window, root's page and the model's context (owner, 2026-10-23: \"locations should be based on a starting EID and we pull from there to render the data what one hop away\"; \"I want logical solution for everything so we aren't inventing new shit\"; \"rendered with render functions not manual wiring\"). What v1 did (~/src/seon/resources/seon/schemas/seon.agent.edn :agent, render/walk.clj neighborhood at :seon.render/distance 1): an entity's schema declares the neighbours a view of it shows as :seon.render/units, attributes in pull's own syntax (a leading _ the reverse ref), components included; the walk pulls the entity and those attributes, and each neighbour renders by its own schema's render. Bring the rule, not v1's 1263 lines (most are SCI caps and caches). Build: (1) :seon.render/units on the schemas this needs: the agent's (:seon.agent/agent-fact: its run :seon.attempt/_agent, its evaluations :seon.cluster.eval/_agent, its root), the run's (:seon.attempt/attempt-fact: its task, its turns :seon.turn/_attempt, the messages to it :seon.message/_to), the task's (its plan, proofs), root's (:seon.root/root-fact: its agents :seon.agent/_root, the open problems); (2) seon.render/walk: one d/pull of an eid with its own attributes and its units (on a branch: the branch's db, or seon.union's U for what spans branches, ids issued once store-wide, docs/storage.md#ids), plus what its string attributes cite (seon.ref/parse, each citation pulled at the depth its delimiter asks, as seon.context pulls it), answering the tree as one map (owner, 2026-10-23: \"We are returning a giant map right? so we can show what rendered it, what it is (a doc a symbol to a file, etc ?)\"): each node its value and what it is (an entity and its schema, a var, a doc section path#anchor, a file path:line, a citation found in a string), where it came from (eid and attribute, or the string and citation that pulled it) and the render that showed it with its :as, so an agent reading it knows what to change: the producer, the render or the citation. Every key in that map is fully namespaced and has a registered schema (owner, 2026-10-23: \"we only use fully namespaced schemas and we don't want to be cute with :you stuff\"): a pulled entity keeps its own attributes, and the walk's own parts are schemas of seon.render (its references, each citation's place, which render made which part). A citation's content is pulled once per context and cited by its number where it appears, so two citations of one var never repeat it; prose is markdown and facts are data (owner, 2026-10-23: \"Somethings are better as data and some are better as markdown\"): a value whose schema is prose (help, a plan, a doc section, a message, a reply) renders as markdown and is parsed for its citations; an entity, proofs, cost, the references and the provenance render as data an agent can read back and use; each value's render decides by its schema, so one evaluation may hold both; (3) the tree printed by the one printer (seon.print with seon.render/nested): the root of the walk by its render :as :block, each unit :as :tile, both faces in one walk (:ai text, :html hiccup), and beside the result which render made which part from which entity and attribute (provenance, data). What is not useful is cut at its producer or given a focused render on its schema, never filtered by a view (owner: \"if shit isn't useful we either cut it at the producer or make specific renders to help focus the context or html\"). Read first: docs/render.md, seon.render (render-of, nested, print-ai), seon.print, seon.ref, seon.context's pulls (what it pulls today and at which depth), v1's walk. Replaces nothing yet: page and evaluations delete the hand-built producers." :fns ["my.agent/source-fn" "seon.context/current-state" "seon.context/found" "seon.context/summary-of" "seon.docs/citation-kind" "seon.docs/cited" "seon.docs/cited-view" "seon.docs/load-dir-tx" "seon.docs/reached" "seon.docs/reference-tokens" "seon.docs/resolve-ref" "seon.docs/source-of" "seon.render/address" "seon.render/cite" "seon.render/entity-schema" "seon.render/entity-schemas" "seon.render/forward" "seon.render/identity-schemas" "seon.render/printed" "seon.render/prose?" "seon.render/render-name" "seon.render/rendered" "seon.render/shown" "seon.render/texts" "seon.render/walk" "seon.turn/act!"] :sections ["docs/references.md#resolving-and-showing" "docs/render.md#the-walk"] :schemas [":seon.agent/description" ":seon.attempt/attempt-fact" ":seon.attempt/reason" ":seon.db/agent-fact" ":seon.db/eval-fact" ":seon.message/body" ":seon.render/address" ":seon.render/ai-by" ":seon.render/ai-text" ":seon.render/attr" ":seon.render/cite-state" ":seon.render/cited-at" ":seon.render/cites" ":seon.render/depth" ":seon.render/eid" ":seon.render/from" ":seon.render/html-by" ":seon.render/kind" ":seon.render/line" ":seon.render/n" ":seon.render/no-entity" ":seon.render/node" ":seon.render/nodes" ":seon.render/place" ":seon.render/ref" ":seon.render/refs" ":seon.render/root" ":seon.render/schema" ":seon.render/shown" ":seon.render/text" ":seon.render/truncated?" ":seon.render/units" ":seon.render/value" ":seon.render/walk" ":seon.root/root-fact" ":seon.task/instructions" ":seon.task/plan" ":seon.task/task-fact" ":seon.turn/reasoning" ":seon.turn/reply"] :tasks ["seon.context/a-cited-var-shows-at-its-citation-depth" "seon.ref/files-namespaces-and-symbols-are-plain-markdown" "seon.repl/source-at-the-repl" "seon.repl/source-reads-the-program" "seon.task/a-task-cites-what-it-needs"]}
{:task "seon.program/an-import-reads-each-form-once" :merge "7d1ecba96" :plan-at "merge" :plan "Done (worktree session reuse, 2026-10-23, task seon.derive/one-content-keyed-derivation's cold paths). seon.source/import-tx of src and test (115 files, 2,409 forms) measured 541M ticks, 3,553 MB and 640k contracted calls cold, about 1.5 MB a form. Where it goes, by phase over the whole tree (warm): Jolt's analyzer 243M ticks and 1,150 MB (~2.6 KB an IR node, 442k nodes); unit-account 75M and 717 MB, of which seon.program/quoted-vars 28M and 492 MB over 140k constants (3.5 KB each: tree-seq, a set and its contract on every keyword and string); account 95M and 697 MB, with macro-uses reading the form's text a second time (27M, 119 MB) and tree-seq walking it (Jolt's tree-seq is ~1.1 KB a node visited); read-forms 34M and 141 MB; account-tx 28M and 148 MB; hasch form ids 17M and 142 MB. Fixed here, at their cause: a scalar constant that is no qualified symbol is not walked (unit-account), and account takes the form as read (:form, required by its contract; seon.source/accounts and seon.repl pass theirs), so macro-uses walks it and the text is read once. After: 497M ticks, 3,213 MB, 519k calls cold (-8%, -10%, -19%). Not fixed here, each its own task: Jolt's analyzer allocation per node (being measured); tree-seq's per-node cost (Jolt's clojure.core); 354 B per armed contracted call (seon.cost/units :fn/armed-call) on hot private helpers (vsym, datum, invoked: 5 calls per IR call node). Replaces the second read; deletes nothing." :fns ["seon.program/account" "seon.program/macro-uses" "seon.program/unit-account" "seon.repl/wrap-record" "seon.source/accounts"] :sections [] :schemas [] :tasks ["seon.program/load-dependencies-leave-quoted-names-out"]}
{:task "seon.source/writeback-order" :merge "6d2312db2" :plan-at "merge" :plan "Found by step E run #10's merge (2026-10-23): an agent defined form-of at its REPL, then redefined summary-of (which keeps its place, seon.program/account-tx) to call it; ```seon.source/file-text``` wrote the forms by :order, so form-of came after its user and the merged file could not load fresh, which blocks every merge that adds a helper. v1 wrote no file (its program lived in the image). Correct: the file's forms are written in the order the image loads them (```seon.program/load-order```, the one in-namespace order: each after the earliest form that defines or declares a var it references). load-order moved forms of files that already load, since :form/refs holds the vars quoted data names (seon.db/install-missing! names 'seon.db/open!, defined after it: 12 of src's files changed under seon.source-test/import-then-write-back-is-a-zero-diff), so an edge between two forms read from a file (:form/source) no longer counts: a file's own order is kept. Replaces the plain sort by :order; deletes nothing. docs/source.md#write-back-mains-files-byte-for-byte." :fns ["seon.merge/key-content" "seon.program/form-row" "seon.program/load-order" "seon.source/file-text"] :sections ["docs/source.md#write-back-mains-files-byte-for-byte"] :schemas [":form/lead"] :tasks ["datahike.query/a-nested-call-in-a-predicate-is-refused" "datahike.query/a-probed-entity-is-sought-not-scanned" "seon.agent/a-resumed-branch-holds-what-main-gained" "seon.agent/an-agent-edits-a-form-by-its-text" "seon.context/the-map-reads-the-program-not-the-process" "seon.program/load-dependencies-leave-quoted-names-out" "seon.program/one-closure-over-a-graph" "seon.source/an-import-reads-only-changed-files" "seon.source/every-test-contract-compiles-where-it-loads"]}
